<section xml:id="section-gaussian">
  <title>The Guassian Integers</title>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-guassian-primes">
    <title>Primes in the Gaussian Integers</title>
    <p>
      So I want to start calculating the primes in <m>\ZZ[i]</m>.
      First, what is the norm? Here <m>D = -1</m>, so the normal is 
      <me>
        N(a + bi) = a^2 + b^2
      </me>
      Note that this norm is always positive, which simplifies the
      investigation. Only <m>0</m> has norm 0, which is always true.
      Next, the units will have norm 1. The only way to get norm 1 is
      to have one of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be 0 and the other 1.
      Therefore, the units are just <m>\ZZ[i]^* = \{1, -1, i, -i\}</m>
    </p>
    <p>
      Now I can talk about primes. I'm going to talk about prime
      numbers here (for reasons I'll prove later) instead of the more
      general prime ideals. The elements of norm two must be primes.
      What are these elements? I can get a norm two by having the
      coefficients both be <m>\pm 1</m> and that is the only way. I
      get this set. 
      <me>  
        \{1 + i, 1 - i, -1 + i, -1 - i \} 
      </me>
      These are all prime, and they are the first four primes.
      However, they aren't really <sq>four</sq> primes. Here are three
      calculations.
      <md>
        <mrow>
          -1(1 + i) = - 1 - i 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          i(1 + i) = i - 1 = -1 + i 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          -i(1 + i) = -i - (-1) = 1 - i
        </mrow>
      </md>
      Once I have the prime <m>(1 + i)</m>, all three other
      <sq>primes</sq> can be constructed by multiplication up to
      units. Primes are only unique up to units (in the usual
      integers, <m>7</m> and <m>-7</m> are considered the same prime.)
      More importantly, multiplication by a unit doesn't
      change the principal ideal: all four of these generate exactly
      the same prime ideal. Therefore, there is only really one prime
      of norm 1, it just can vary up to units. 
    </p>
    <p>
      I move on to higher norms. Norm three is impossible: <m>a^2 +
      b^2 = 3</m> is not true for any integer inputs. So the next norm
      to consider is four. Here are all the elements of norm four.
      <me>
        \{2, -2, 2i, -2i\}
      </me>
      Some of these might be prime and some might be products of the
      previous primes. How do I check? Well, the most obvious way is
      the same way I check for the integers: look at the products of
      smaller primes. If I investigate the products of the smaller
      primes, I can find the following identity. 
      <me>
        2 = (1+i)(1-i) = -i(1+i)(1+i) = -i (1+i)^2
      </me>
      This is a factorization of the number 2 (more formally, the
      Gaussian integer <m>(2 + 0i)</m>) into primes. In the Gaussian
      integers, 2 is no longer a prime. When a prime from <m>\ZZ</m>
      is no longer prime in a larger ring, we say that the prime
      <term>splits</term> in the larger ring. In the Gaussian
      integers, 2 splits. 
    </p>
    <p>
      What about the other three? Well, the other three are all 2
      multiplied by a unit and primes are only unique up to units. So
      none of the elements of norm 4 are prime: they all factor as the
      square of the prime <m>(1 + i)</m>, up to multiplication by
      units. 
    </p>
    <p>
      The next norm is five. There are eight elements of norm five. 
      <me>
        \{\pm 1 \pm 2i, \pm 2 \pm i\}
      </me>
      Up to multiplication by units, there are only two elements here. 
      <me>
        \{1 + 2i, 1 - 2i\}
      </me>
      I claim that both of these are prime. Why? Well, I only have one
      prime so far, which has norm 2. The square of that prime has
      norm 4, and its cube has norm 8. I can never get a norm of 5
      this way, so an element of norm 5 cannot be the product of
      smaller non-units. Therefore, these two elements of norm 5 must
      both be prime. 
    </p>
    <p>
      There are no elements of norm 6 or 7: these numbers cannot be
      produced by the norm formul <m>a^2 + b^2</m>. So the next norm
      is norm 8. Up to units there is only one element of norm 8. 
      <me>
        \{2 + 2i\}
      </me>
      This factors pretty clearly: <m>2 + 2i = 2(1 + i)</m>, and I
      already know how 2 factors. Therefore I have a prime
      factorization of this element: <m>2 + 2i = -i(1 + i)^3</m>. This
      is not a prime.
    </p>
    <p>
      I can keep doing this for a while. Here is a table of the
      progress through the next few norms. Everything is unique up to
      multiplication by units. I skip over the norms which have no
      elements. Note that any compositie integer, such as 4, cannot be
      prime in any larger ring since the factorization in the integers
      still holds. 
    </p>
    <me>
      \begin{array}{c| c c c c c c c }
        \text{Norm} \amp \text{Element} \amp \text{Prime?} \\
        \hline
        9 \amp 3 + 0i \amp \text{Yes} \\
        10 \amp 3 + i \amp \text{No} \\
        10 \amp 3 - i \amp \text{No} \\
        13 \amp 3 + 2i \amp \text{Yes} \\
        13 \amp 3 - 2i \amp \text{Yes} \\
        16 \amp 4 + 0i \amp \text{No} \\
        17 \amp 4 + i \amp \text{Yes} \\
        17 \amp 4 - i \amp \text{Yes} \\
        18 \amp 3 + 3i \amp \text{No} \\
        20 \amp 4 + 2i \amp \text{No} \\ 
        20 \amp 4 - 2i \amp \text{No} \\ 
        25 \amp 5 + 0i \amp \text{No} \\
        25 \amp 4 + 3i \amp \text{No} \\
        25 \amp 4 - 3i \amp \text{No} 
      \end{array}
    </me>
    <p>
      Note that we get another split prime: 5 is no longer prime. Here
      is the factorization: <m>5 = (2 + i)(2 - i)</m>. I could keep
      going, but hopefully there is enough here. If there is enough, I
      could start to ask: is there some pattern here? If you stare
      long enough, perhaps after calcluating a bit more, you may be
      able to guess the following proposition. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Up to multiplication by units, the primes in the Gaussian
          integers fit one of the two following patterns. 
          <ul>
            <li>
              <m>a + bi = p + 0i</m> where <m>p</m> is a usual prime
              in the integers which is congruent to 3 modulo 4.   
            </li>
            <li>
              <m>a^2 + b^2 = p</m> where <m>p</m> is prime as an
              element of the normal integers. (It will turn out that
              this prime can never be congruent to 3 modulo 4.) 
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </proposition>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-gaussian-division">
    <title>Division Algorithm in the Gaussian Integers</title>
    <p>
      In <xref ref="theorem-division-algorithm"/>, I stated the
      division algorithm. This is an important proprety of the
      integers. It's also the way in which gcds and lcms are usually
      calculated. Most importantly, if two numbers share a factor,
      particular a prime factor, then the division algorithm can find
      this factor. This fact makes the division algorithm very useful
      for investigating primes and prime decomposition. 
    </p>
    <p>
      This is yet another place where I can ask for extensions from
      the integers to other rings: which rings still have a division
      algorithm? It turns out that the division algorithm works in the
      Gaussian integers, which I will now demonstrate. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Before stating the proposition, let me point something out. The
      division algorithm for the integers works only because the
      remainder is <em>smaller</em> than the divisor. Smaller in the
      integers means in absolute value with the usual ordering of the
      integers. As pointed out before, most rings do not have a
      natural order on them, but we can define a norm to make an
      order. Therefore, division algorithms will depend on the norm I
      use. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <title>Division Algorithm for the Gaussian Integers</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>a = a_1 + a_2i</m> and <m>b = b_1 + b_2 i</m> be
          Gaussian integers with <m>b \neq 0</m>. Then there exists
          Gausian integers <m>q - q_1 + q_2i</m> and <m>r = r_1 +
          r_2i</m> such that these two facts are true. 
          <ul>
            <li>
              <m>a = bq +  r</m>
            </li>
            <li>
              <m>N(r) \lt N(b)</m>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          There are several method of constructing this algorithm. The
          most efficient uses the arithmetic of the complex numbers,
          since Gaussian integers are themselves complex numbers. Some
          piece of this may be unfamiliar to you, but let me at least
          give you the idea of the proof. 
        </p>
        <p>
          Since these are complex numbers, I can divide by the
          non-zero number <m>b</m>. (This division is happening in
          <m>\CC</m>, not <m>\ZZ[i]</m>, but I'l relate it back to the
          ring eventually.) Therefore <m>\frac{a}{b}</m> is some complex
          number. The complex numbers have a notion of length between
          them and this length is actually given by the square root of
          the norm I am using for the Gaussian integers. Therefore, let
          <m>q</m> be another Guassian integer which is less than
          length one away from <m>\frac{a}{b}</m>. (This is always
          possible due to the distribution of the Guassian integers
          among the complex number.) Then I also define <m>r = a -
          bq</m>. This is still a Gaussian integer, since it is formed
          from three other Gaussian integers using normal arithmetic. 
        </p>
        <p>
          With these definitions, let me check the properties. First,
          the definition of <m>r</m> rearranged already gives the
          desired equation <m>a = bq + r</m>. Now I need the norms. I
          calculate the norm of the remainder.
          <me>
            N(r) = N(a - bq) = N\left( b \left( \frac{a}{b} - q
            \right) \right) = N(b) N\left( \frac{a}{b} - q \right) 
          </me>
          But I choose <m>q</m> precise with the property that it
          within one unit from <m>\frac{a}{b}</m>. This means that the
          second term here is less than one. 
          <me>
            N(r) = N(b) N\left( \frac{a}{b} - q \right) \lt N(b) 
          </me>
          This completes the proof. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p> 
      So now I know that the Gaussian integers have a division
      algorithm. How does this help me? Well, I'm going to move away
      from the Guassian integers for a brief section to talk about
      what going on in general. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
